---
import "../styles/global.css";

const canonical = new URL("/", Astro.site).href;
const title = "Linux Systems & Network Administrator · Computer Engineer CV";
const description =
  "Computer engineer from Camagüey, Cuba. Six years as systems and network administrator at Cuba's National Radar Center; now solar tracking field service in the United States. Linux, networks, servers, SQL Server.";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <meta
      name="keywords"
      content="Linux systems administrator, network administrator, computer engineer, sysadmin, solar tracking, field service, SQL Server, Camagüey Cuba"
    />
    <link rel="icon" href="/assets/site/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="canonical" href={canonical} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="profile" />
    <meta property="og:url" content={canonical} />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content="Six years running systems and networks at Cuba's National Radar Center. Now solar tracking field service in the US. A charcoal figure walks the whole line."
    />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <canvas id="world" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
    <div class="track" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    {/*
      The drawing is the experience, but a CV that exists only inside a canvas is
      invisible to a search engine and to a screen reader. The same content lives
      here as real text.
    */}
    <main class="readable">
      <article>
        <h1>Computer engineer · Linux systems and network administrator</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Six years keeping critical infrastructure online. Systems and network
          administrator at Cuba's National Radar Center, now solar tracking field
          service in the United States. Looking for systems, network or Linux
          work — remote or in the field. <a href="mailto:terrerov@gmail.com"
            >terrerov@gmail.com</a
          >
        </p>

        <h2>Experience</h2>

        <section>
          <h3>Systems and network administrator</h3>
          <p class="place">
            National Radar Center, Provincial Meteorology Center · Camagüey,
            Cuba · six years
          </p>
          <p>
            Systems and network administrator at Cuba's National Radar Center.
            Servers, links, and a weather service that depended on all of it
            staying up.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Linux administration</li><li>Networks</li><li>Servers</li><li>
              Radar infrastructure
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h3>Computer science specialist</h3>
          <p class="place">University of Camagüey, Economics Department</p>
          <p>
            SQL Server and the accounting systems for a whole university
            department. If a number moved on that campus, it went through me.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>SQL Server</li><li>Databases</li><li>Internal systems</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h3>Solar tracking field service</h3>
          <p class="place">Solar farms · United States</p>
          <p>
            Solar tracking systems across US solar farms — the mechanics and the
            logic that turn every row to follow the sun all day.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Solar tracking</li><li>Field service</li><li>Troubleshooting</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h3>Trades and kitchens</h3>
          <p class="place">United States</p>
          <p>
            Construction, remodeling, parking lots, delivery routes. Then
            kitchens: Wingstop, Cuban and Italian. A kitchen on a Friday night
            teaches what an outage teaches: prioritize, do not freeze, get the
            plate out. You read a system differently once you have carried it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h2>Education</h2>

        <section>
          <h3>Computer engineering</h3>
          <p class="place">University of Camagüey · five years</p>
          <p>
            Informatics and exact sciences over five years. Where the trade
            started, and where I learned to sit with a problem until it moved. I
            became a father in my fourth year — exams and a newborn in the same
            week. Nothing since has felt busy.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h2>The line</h2>

        <section>
          <p>
            I grew up in Florida, Camagüey, a small town in the middle of Cuba.
            We moved often enough that I lost count of the schools; the studying
            stuck. After the radar center I packed one suitcase for the United
            States — new country, no contacts, no shortcuts. The trade came with
            me.
          </p>
          <p>
            Free software was the night workshop the whole time. Job site or
            kitchen line, I am still a sysadmin in my head.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h2>Skills</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Linux, open source, Bash</li>
          <li>Networking and network administration</li>
          <li>Server and systems administration</li>
          <li>SQL Server and databases</li>
          <li>Solar tracking systems, field service and troubleshooting</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <section>
          <p>
            I am looking for systems, network or Linux work — remote or in the
            field. One email is enough: <a href="mailto:terrerov@gmail.com"
              >terrerov@gmail.com</a
            >
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </main>

    <script>
      import "../scripts/main.js";
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
